/* ---------- 9. Q_RACE — AI race section ---------- */
var Q_RACE = {
  position: 'The AI race may be rational for each actor individually but dangerous collectively. The central question is whether humanity can turn competition into capability while still preserving control, trust, labour stability, and democratic legitimacy.',
  sections: [
    { h:'Why countries race',                    d:'Frontier AI looks like the next general-purpose technology. Governments fear strategic, economic, and military disadvantage if a competitor leads. Domestic politics rewards visible action; "we are racing" is easier to sell than "we are coordinating".' },
    { h:'Why companies race',                    d:'First-movers shape standards, capture customers, and attract capital. Investor expectations and shareholder pressure compound the incentive. Slowing down unilaterally costs share without slowing the technology.' },
    { h:'Why slowing down is hard',              d:'Each actor faces a competitive penalty for slowing alone. There is no shared verification regime to confirm others slow with you. Without trust and verification, racing is the dominant strategy even when everyone would prefer coordination.' },
    { h:'Why coordination is hard',              d:'Different jurisdictions hold different values and threat models. The technology is dual-use, fast-moving, and partially open. Verification is genuinely difficult. None of these makes coordination impossible — only hard.' },
    { h:'What could go wrong',                   d:'Capability outruns evaluation; concentration of power; eroded labour stability; weakened democratic legitimacy; accelerated misuse; and reduced societal capacity to course-correct. Each is plausible; none is automatic.' },
    { h:'What could go right',                   d:'Cheap intelligence broadens access to expertise; productivity gains fund stronger safety nets; hard problems in medicine, science, and education become tractable; and competition produces both capability and pressure to improve safety. None of these is automatic either.' },
    { h:'What winning should actually mean',     d:'Winning is producing the most useful intelligence per watt, dollar, and unit of trust — at speed, with control, with broad legitimacy, and without fracturing the societies that built it. Anything less is a category error sold as victory.' }
  ]
};

